  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1257 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nWeak disturbances moving along a broad upper level trof over\nour region will continue 20-60% chances of showers and\nthunderstorms into early this evening, with activity focused\nprimarily across southeast Missouri and southern Illinois. Late\ntonight should be mostly dry, then any additional convection \nwill be pretty isolated Saturday into Saturday evening. Due to \nthe isolated/scattered nature of the the showers and storms, \nrainfall amounts will range from zero to a half inch, with \nlocalized higher amounts possible.\n\nThe heat and humidity will be ramping up a little bit for the\nnext several days as broad upper level ridging over the\nsouthwestern U.S. expands farther east, and due to persistent \nsouth to southwest flow at the surface. On Saturday, high \ntemperatures will reach around 90 degrees with afternoon heat \nindices around 100 degrees. Conditions will be dry with lots of \nsunshine Sunday through Tuesday, with highs in the 90 to around\n95 degree range, and heat indices from 100 to 108 degrees. It \nis possible we could need a heat advisory by late in the weekend\ninto early next week.\n\nBy mid week, the upper ridge will be weakening, and models show\na frontal boundary setting up north of the PAH forecast area, \nsinking slowly south into our region through late week. Chances\nof showers and storms will gradually spread from northeast to \nsouthwest Tuesday night into Wednesday. Precipitation chances\nthen hover at 40-60% from Wednesday through Friday morning. \nSome decent rainfall will be possible due to the very slow \nprogression of the cold front, with current forecast amounts of\nat least an inch. Of course, this is pretty far out in the \nforecast, and much fine tuning will be needed.\n\nWith the cold front and rain/clouds, temperatures by Thursday \nand Friday will drop off to several degrees below normal.",
